\import{fcap-macros}
\meta{agent-authored}{true}

\tag{fcap}
\tag{spin}
\tag{clifford}

\taxon{Proposition}
\title{A Lorentz frame can be prescribed}

\p{Let #{V} be four-dimensional over #{\mathbb R}, and let #{Q} be a
nondegenerate quadratic form with positive and negative indices #{(1,3)}.
Suppose

##{Q(t)=1,\qquad Q(s)=-1,\qquad B_Q(t,s)=0.}

There is an isometry #{e:(\mathbb R^4,Q_{1,3})\simeq(V,Q)} with
#{e(e_0)=t} and #{e(e_1)=s}. Moreover,
#{H=\operatorname{span}(t,s)} has an orthogonal complement of dimension two
on which #{Q} is negative definite. The sign convention for #{Q_{1,3}} is
the one fixed in \ref{fcap-0011}.}

\subtree{
\taxon{Proof}
\p{For all #{a,b\in\mathbb R}, polarization gives

##{Q(at+bs)=a^2-b^2.}

The polar Gram matrix of #{t,s} is #{\operatorname{diag}(2,-2)}, so they are
independent and #{H} is regular of inertia #{(1,1)}. By
\citet{Section 7, no. 2, Theorem 1 and Definition 2, pp. 117--118}{bourbaki2007algebra},
the real law of inertia gives an initial isometry
#{j:(\mathbb R^4,Q_{1,3})\simeq(V,Q)}.
The isometry from #{j(\operatorname{span}(e_0,e_1))} to #{H} sending
#{j(e_0)} to #{t} and #{j(e_1)} to #{s} has regular source, so Witt extension
supplies an ambient isometry #{g} of #{(V,Q)}
\leanref/tauceti{QuadraticMap.IsometryEquiv.exists_extension}. Then
#{e=g\circ j} has the prescribed values.}

\p{The orthogonal decomposition has summands #{H} and #{H^\perp}.
Additivity of the positive and negative indices
\leanref/tauceti{QuadraticForm.sigPos_prod}
\leanref/tauceti{QuadraticForm.sigNeg_prod} gives

##{(1,3)=(1,1)+(0,2).}

Hence #{H^\perp} is the required negative plane.}
}

\p{The isometry transport in \ref{fcap-0029} carries the standard first two
Clifford generators to this prescribed pair and commutes with the Spin vector
action. The hyperbolic split and quaternionic matrix calculation are already
in \ref{fcap-0026}, which yields the abstract model
#{\Cl(V,Q)\simeq M_2(\mathbb H)}. This does not identify a particular
gamma-matrix homomorphism, its kernel, or its image.}
